+    "position": 1238,
+    "side": "RIGHT",
+    "body": "### Database Architecture\n**Risk:** `db.prepare(...)` is called inside `moveToCommons()` on every invocation to execute `SELECT is_treasury FROM members WHERE public_key = ?`. In `better-sqlite3`, invoking `db.prepare()` inline inside a frequently called function recompiles the SQL statement and allocates statement handles on every call, causing unnecessary GC pressure and CPU overhead in the hot transaction execution path.\n\n**Suggested Fix:**\nMove statement preparation to top-level module scope so SQLite compiles it once:\n```ts\nconst stmtGetIsTreasury = db.prepare('SELECT is_treasury FROM members WHERE public_key = ?');\n\nexport function moveToCommons(\n    from: string,\n    amount: number,\n    memo: string,\n    opts?: { allowMemberDebit?: boolean },\n): Transaction | null {\n    const synthetic = isSyntheticAccount(from);\n    const treasury = !synthetic\n        && (stmtGetIsTreasury.get(from) as any)?.is_treasury === 1;\n```"
+  },
+  {
+    "path": "apps/server/src/state-engine.ts",
+    "position": 2677,
+    "side": "RIGHT",
+    "body": "### Database Architecture\n**Risk:** In `adminPruneUser`, `payFromCommons` and `moveToCommons` are called inside a `db.transaction(() => { ... })()` closure. Both functions immediately mutate in-memory ledger state (`ledger.moveToCommons` / `ledger.deductFromCommons` and global `COMMONS_BALANCE`) before running SQLite DML statements. If a subsequent SQL query in `adminPruneUser` fails or throws an exception (such as `adminSetUserStatus` or updating `posts`), SQLite will roll back database mutations, but in-memory ledger balances will NOT roll back. This leaves in-memory state permanently desynchronized from the SQLite database, breaking mutual credit zero-sum conservation invariants.\n\n**Suggested Fix:**\nWrap the transaction in a try-catch block that resynchronizes in-memory ledger state from SQLite via `reconcileLedgerFromDb()` if an error occurs and the transaction rolls back:\n```ts\nexport function adminPruneUser(publicKey: string) {\n    try {\n        db.transaction(() => {\n            const account = ledger.getAccount(publicKey);\n            const balance = account.balance;\n\n            if (balance < 0) {\n                const D = Math.abs(balance);\n                payFromCommons(publicKey, D, `Settle bad debt for pruned user: ${publicKey}`, { allowDeficit: true });\n            } else if (balance > 0) {\n                moveToCommons(publicKey, balance, `Confiscate credit for pruned user: ${publicKey}`,\n                    { allowMemberDebit: true });\n            }\n\n            adminSetUserStatus(publicKey, 'pruned');\n            db.prepare(\"UPDATE posts SET status='cancelled', active=0 WHERE author_pubkey=? AND status IN ('active', 'pending')\").run(publicKey);\n        })();\n    } catch (err) {\n        reconcileLedgerFromDb();\n        throw err;\n    }\n    broadcast({ type: 'user_pruned', publicKey });\n}\n```"
+  },
+  {
+    "path": "apps/server/src/routes/treasury.ts",
+    "position": 238,
+    "side": "RIGHT",
+    "body": "### Database Architecture\n**Risk:** The sweep route in `treasury.ts` checks treasury balance via `getBalance(treasury).balance` prior to invoking `moveToCommons(treasury, amt, ...)`. `moveToCommons` writes transactions and updates account balance rows without transactional isolation or a Compare-And-Swap (CAS) guard against concurrent requests. Concurrent API requests targeting the same treasury could pass balance checks simultaneously and race to debit the treasury, causing unexpected failures or balance mismatches.\n\n**Suggested Fix:**\nEnsure the balance validation and `moveToCommons` execution are executed within an atomic block or CAS check so concurrent sweep requests are properly serialized."
+  }
+]
